
# ^ Conventional Commit: type(scope): subject
#
#   Write the subject for someone reading the release notes. The commit IS the
#   version signal and the changelog entry -- there is no separate changeset step.
#
# Types that trigger a release:
#   feat:      new feature            -> minor  (patch while 0.x)
#   fix:       bug fix                -> patch
#   perf:      performance fix        -> patch
#
# Types that do NOT trigger a release:
#   docs: refactor: test: chore: ci: build: style: revert:
#
# Breaking a public API? Add "!" after the type (feat!: ...) OR a footer:
#
#   BREAKING CHANGE: <what broke and what to do about it>
#
#   The api-contract check fails the PR if the public API changed by more than
#   your commits declare -- it tells you exactly what to add. See VERSIONING.md.
#
# commitlint (the commit-msg hook) rejects a message that isn't a Conventional
# Commit. Use `git commit --no-verify` only for a throwaway WIP checkpoint.
